Exclusive canonicalization goldens.

Each NN-name.xml is an input fixture and the matching NN-name.exc is what
xmllint produced for it with --exc-c14n. c14n.golden.spec.ts asserts this
plugin's own toCanonicalXml reproduces those bytes exactly.

The .exc files come from an implementation this project did not author, and they
were produced before the walker existed. That is the point: the spec compares our
output against libxml2 rather than against itself. Never edit a .exc file by hand,
or the corpus stops proving anything.

To regenerate, from the plugin root:

  pnpm goldens

That runs scripts/regenerate-c14n-goldens.mjs, which needs xmllint on PATH and
rewrites every .exc file plus this one. Canonical output can differ between
libxml2 releases, so regenerating moves the baseline. Do it only when you mean
to, and run the suite afterwards.

xmllint exposes neither an InclusiveNamespaces PrefixList nor node-set selection,
so any golden needing those comes from lxml or xmlsec1 instead and is recorded
with the fixtures that use it.

